    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{a+b}{a-b}=\\sqrt2$.\n\nComplete the middle terms to obtain $(a+b)^2=8ab$ and $(a-b)^2=4ab$. Divide and take the positive square root since $a>b>0$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(a+b)^2&=a^2+b^2+2ab\\\\(a+b)^2&=6ab+2ab\\\\(a+b)^2&=8ab,\\\\(a-b)^2&=a^2+b^2-2ab\\\\(a-b)^2&=6ab-2ab\\\\(a-b)^2&=4ab.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $ab>0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left(\\frac{a+b}{a-b}\\right)^2&=\\frac{8ab}{4ab}\\\\\\left(\\frac{a+b}{a-b}\\right)^2&=2.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth $a+b$ and $a-b$ are positive because $a>b>0$. Therefore\n$$\\frac{a+b}{a-b}=\\sqrt2.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
